[Music]  money doesn't grow on trees  but chopping them down has helped lots  of the world get richer  since the beginning of the 20th century  humans have been raiding these resources  almost as though they were Limitless  the problem is the world is now paying a  heavy environmental price  it certainly looks like a crisis after  the worst drought in 80 years so is  there a financial solution if we don't  change the movement of money we'll  continue to finance ourselves into  Extinction is paying for trees to be  protected and restored the answer and  how do you put a price on their worth  foreign  to understand the alarming story of  deforestation let's start with Costa  Rica in the 1980s  [Music]  Maria's family was typical of many  enticed by The Riches of Agriculture her  father cleared and converted their  Forest to pasture  foreign  markets with the driving force behind  deforestation  from 1940 to 1987 the proportion of land  in Costa Rica covered by forests  plummeted from 75 percent to 21 percent  Costa Rica's experience is far from  unique  the rest of the world jumped on the  bandwagon too  humans have been cutting trees for  thousands of years but there was a  massive acceleration in the last century  the U.N estimates around 10 million  hectares of forest are being lost each  year  that's around 27 football fields every  minute  fewer trees means less carbon dioxide  being absorbed from the atmosphere  and their destruction only adds more  emissions accelerating climate change  The Heart Of This Global crisis sits the  world's largest rainforest and the  so-called Trump of the tropics jair  bolsonaro  um  from 2019 to 2022 the rate of  deforestation in Brazil Rose by roughly  50 percent attracting critics around the  world  the problem is it's a bit Rich for  wealthy countries to demand that poorer  ones give up the economic benefits of  deforestation  the majority of deforestation in recent  years has really been driven by  agriculture expansion in developing  countries it can almost seem  hypocritical to criticize these  developing nations for following a  pathway that effectively made rich  countries wealthy in previous centuries  so what can be done  the simple answer is for rich countries  to incentivize poorer ones to protect  and restore their forests  one thing the wealthy world has tried is  stumping up cash for new tree planting  schemes but many of these haven't borne  fruit tree planting programs are  incredibly popular they can seem almost  childishly easy you plant a tree you  save the climate the reality of course  is nowhere near that simple in terms of  the climate it can be far more  beneficial to conserve the forest that's  there  so another way is to focus rewards for  developing countries on conserving  existing forests it works like this  using historic rates of deforestation a  country predicts how much Forest is  likely to lose in the future  any Forest that it manages to conserve  is judged against this Benchmark the  donor hands over a financial reward to  the developing country for the trees it  saved  paying a sum for each ton of carbon  dioxide that would have otherwise been  emitted  it's not just the developing country  that stands to gain the donors often  rich countries or companies can benefit  too in exchange for their money they can  claim what's called a carbon credit  which they can use to offset their  emissions elsewhere  Carmen credits are a way of allowing  companies countries people who can't  reduce their own emissions currently to  offset those emissions elsewhere they  are seen as an important part of  transitioning towards a net zero economy  I started out hugging orangutans but now  I find myself hugging asset managers and  bankers a bit more often Andrew Mitchell  is a zoologist turned financial advisor  he encourages investors to buy carbon  credits and believes they're an  important tool for Forest conservation  these credits are hugely valuable it's a  transition strategy whilst you're  reducing your carbon emissions you have  to buy offsets you should buy offsets  it sounds like a win-win but so far the  results are mixed Shady players have  gained the system and pulled dirty  tricks like duplicating credits or  claiming to conserve land never under  threat or moving the problem to the  forest next door  built into the Paris agreement is this  consensus that there should be a global  scheme for exchanging carbon credits  across borders in practice the rules and  standards that would govern such a  global market have been incredibly  difficult to establish and so right now  what we've got is a system which is  really unregulated and although  standards and the scale of carbon credit  schemes are improving there's still one  big sticking point the rewards for  conserving trees still lag behind those  for deforesting  we need carbon markets to grow they  won't really achieve their full  potential unless the price of offsetting  a ton of carbon is competitive with the  profits that can be generated from other  activities on that land ultimately  conservation has to become profitable  re-enter Costa Rica  once the poster child of deforestation  but now a global leader in conservation  and an example of how to make saving  nature pay  here landowners are paid by the  government to restore and conserve  forests  and this has helped to create new and  sustainable economic opportunities  [Music]  remember Maria whose father made a  living out of chopping down trees like  many in Costa Rica her family have since  branched out  and now do the very opposite  [Music]  Maria and her grandchildren we plant  forests so far 350 hectares worth  the government pays them and many other  families around 80 a year for each  hectare of forest that's restored or  kept intact  Graces  Ramos  [Music]  it might sound like a government subsidy  scheme but here's the key bit it's not  it's a system known as payments for  Environmental Services launched in the  late 90s is paid for by citizens who are  seen as benefiting from the services  provided by healthy forests like carbon  storage the protection of vital food  chains and clean water the money is not  coming from  from the national budget the money comes  from those who benefit  Carlos Manuel Rodriguez is Costa Rica's  former environment Minister and the  brains behind the scheme  which is paid for by a levy on consumers  water bills and attacks on their fuel  use  Costa Rica was the first developing  country that put a tax on on carbon to  generate a revenue for the payment of  Environmental Services everybody who  uses fuel in Costa Rica and pollutes the  environment contributing to climate  change must pay in the price of  purchases that fuel an extra amount that  goes to this foreign fund that pays  Farmers so far the scheme has invested  more than 500 million dollars in the  conservation of forests  and it's paid off in the space of a  generation Forest covering Costa Rica  jumped from 21 to more than 50 percent  with the restoration of trees have come  new opportunities for profitable and  sustainable Industries  blowing away the idea that conservation  and economic development are always at  loggerheads nature-based tourism has  become naturally important because of  our jobs in rural Costa Rica as opposed  to what we had in the past which was  this irrational  unsustainable agricultural production  there is no dichotomy between  preservation and economic development  since 1987 the peak of deforestation in  Costa Rica the country's GDP has risen  by an average of 4.2 percent a year  Costa Rica isn't a magic formula for  saving the world's forests  since it began prioritizing conservation  it's increased food imports an option  not available to all countries  but there is a lot Costa Rica can teach  others for one thing it's improved some  of its bad spending habits reversing  subsidies that incentivize the clearance  of land at all costs a lead more  countries should follow we should put  more money into the protection of nature  from governments but the reality is even  if we doubled all that money it wouldn't  come close to beating the subsidies that  directly cause harm to Nature each year  the world spends around 150 billion  dollars on protecting nature  but around 12 times that on subsidies  that harm it  some campaigners argue a more radical  fix is needed to save the volume of  forests necessary to avert environmental  catastrophe  they say what's required is a  fundamental shift away from society's  focus on profits and growth the problem  with GDP is that it measures everything  we build and nothing that we protect  it's all beginning to change now people  are waking up to the importance of  keeping nature going to keep our economy  going not everyone supports such a  drastic change to economic thinking but  most do agree that change is not  happening fast enough  with the eyes of many global leaders on  other challenges the world is facing  multiple crises in the midst of all this  there is a risk that nature conservation  and the climate crisis are demoted in  the pecking order but climate change the  global climate does not wait for  short-term crises to pass  foreign  bullet that will reverse climate change  the world needs to urgently reduce  industrial emissions  and prioritize policies to decarbonize  economies but unless trees are worth  more alive than dead deforestation and  its Devastation will continue  I'm Tom standage Deputy editor of The  Economist and editor of the world ahead  to read more of our future gazing  coverage click on the link thanks for  watching and don't forget to subscribe

